WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.269 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Sometimes these
movements have to be driven

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:05.805
by people who are
true believers,

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:08.341
because what happens is they
push things all the way over

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.609
and then it comes back.

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:11.611
Americans are not
an extreme people.

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:13.146
We are not extreme.

00:00:13.146 --> 00:00:14.814
Americans are not socialist.

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:17.484
Americans are not
super right wing.

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:19.519
Americans want a better
life for their kids,

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:21.488
for the most part,
and are too busy

00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:24.758
with their everyday thing.

00:00:24.758 --> 00:00:26.693 align:start size:97% position:3%
I gotta take my kid to
baseball and I want to be able

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:29.229
to afford after school
activities for my kids.

00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:30.930
That's what they
really care about.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.465
I want a little extra cash.

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:35.068
(gentle music)

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:38.371 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin, and this
is, "The Rubin Report."

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:40.840 line:0%
As always guys, click
that subscribe button

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.509 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
and that little
notification bell,

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:45.478
so you have a small chance at
least of seeing our videos,

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:48.348
and joining me today is
a comedian, an actor,

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:51.785 align:start size:97% position:3%
and co-host of, "The Fighter
and the Kid," podcast.

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:53.953
Brian Callen, welcome
to, "The Rubin Report.

00:00:53.953 --> 00:00:55.321
- Thank you, sir.

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:56.790 align:start size:94% position:6%
Big fan, big fan, love it.
- Look at us.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.158 align:start size:81% position:19%
Big fan both ways.
- Here I am,

00:00:58.158 --> 00:01:00.260 align:start size:97% position:3%
I finally made it.
- You're in the internet now.

00:01:01.094 --> 00:01:02.629
This is like the center

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:04.097
of the internet, my garage.
- No liability here at all.

00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:05.365
Yes, this right here.

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:06.766
- Everything that we
say here stays here

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.201 align:start size:84% position:16%
and no one will
get in any trouble.

00:01:08.201 --> 00:01:09.736 align:start size:88% position:13%
You're completely fine.
- Yeah, exactly.

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:12.772
As long as you, as long as
you don't express yourself

00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:15.909 align:start size:94% position:6%
freely, and thoughtfully,
and with reason.

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:17.577
- That seems like a
good place to start.

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:19.245 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah.
- Comedy in an age

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:21.614
of cancellation, how are
you feeling these days?

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:26.619 align:start position:0% line:0%
- I think that it feels as
though this is the last bastion

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:31.591 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
for satire, honest expression,
original self-expression.

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:33.493 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, I've had
comics call me and say,

00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:35.595
"Well, I've got this show
with a certain network,

00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:38.198
"and I'm afraid
that what I said,

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:40.900 align:start size:97% position:3%
"I expressed a point of view
that was more complicated

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.137 align:start size:88% position:13%
"than some black or
white," no pun intended,

00:01:44.137 --> 00:01:44.971 align:start size:81% position:19%
you know?
- Oh you're going

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:46.239
for it already.

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:48.508
- And should I,
should I do this?

00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:51.511
You have comics now wondering
if they should edit themselves

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:56.449 align:start size:94% position:6%
in podcasts, and my answer
is if that is, what,

00:01:56.449 --> 00:02:00.820
if you are worried about that,

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.555 align:start size:84% position:16%
because you're working
for that network,

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:04.124 align:start size:84% position:16%
then you shouldn't
work for that network,

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:05.792
then you're no longer a comic.

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:07.127 align:start size:84% position:16%
Then you're no longer
in the business

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:09.963
of original self expression.

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:14.968 align:start size:97% position:3%
Thoughts and art has always
been there to disturb,

00:02:15.702 --> 00:02:16.970
to shake things up.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.205
Let's take, "Will and
Grace," as an example.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:21.474
I auditioned for that
show maybe five times.

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:23.877
I can't remember if it was
for Jack, if it was for Eric,

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:25.545
if it was for both.

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:27.313
And I knew it was
gonna be a hit,

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:30.316 align:start size:97% position:3%
but it was so groundbreaking,
it was so astonishing.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:33.286 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was the first time
that gay people were kind

00:02:33.286 --> 00:02:38.291
of highlighted as this, as
people with senses of humor

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:40.994
who wanted the same thing
that straight people want.

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:45.732 line:0%
I remember that being
a radical new idea,

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:48.902 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
and it, you know,
back then, you know,

00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:50.770 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
we always talk about
how you can get canceled

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.672
for certain words, you say.

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:56.676 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, in the, well, let's
say all, through all

00:02:56.676 --> 00:03:00.713
of American history up
through probably the 90s,

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:03.383
if you were found
out to be gay in a,

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:06.119
in the workplace, well,
if you didn't get fired,

00:03:06.119 --> 00:03:07.620
you weren't gonna get promoted.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.591
It was a major liability
and physically dangerous.

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:16.596 align:start size:84% position:16%
So along comes, "Will
and Grace," and a
network took a risk.

00:03:17.931 --> 00:03:20.200
So in that sense,
you know, you can,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.336
you can point to examples
where it's very important

00:03:23.336 --> 00:03:24.904
to continue to speak your mind,

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:28.141
and it's very important
when the pendulum swings

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:31.911
too far right, or too far left,
or too far in any direction.

00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:35.448
As an artist, you gotta push
back, you gotta kind of go,

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:36.883
you gotta make fun of it,

00:03:36.883 --> 00:03:39.719 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that's the one,
through all this insanity,

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:41.454
and there are a lot of
bad ideas out there,

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:43.356
And there are a lot of
sort of like, there's,

00:03:43.356 --> 00:03:44.624
we're very polarized.

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:46.259
There's no room for
nuance, as you know,

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:48.428
as I'm sure you've picked up on,

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:52.599 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I do think Americans
have this really great safety

00:03:52.599 --> 00:03:54.500 align:start size:84% position:16%
valve, this switch,
which is humor.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.236
We tend to make
fun of ourselves.

00:03:56.236 --> 00:03:59.372
We tend to make fun of the,
of cancel culture already.

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:02.141
We tend to make fun of,
of all of these things.

00:04:03.243 --> 00:04:05.712 align:start size:88% position:13%
There's, Trump is always
being made fun of,

00:04:05.712 --> 00:04:10.516 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so is the other
side, so I think at least

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:15.521 align:start size:97% position:3%
we are going to always,
we'll always have that thing.

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:20.193 align:start size:88% position:13%
Even kids now, my
friend's son who is 16,

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:23.796 align:start size:97% position:3%
my buddy said, "Look at
that, look at that butterfly,

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:26.633
"I wonder what he's
gonna land on next."

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:29.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
And his friend said,
"Dad, how are you assuming

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:30.803
"that's a he?"

00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:32.405
I mean, how do you know?

00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:33.640 align:start size:75% position:25%
And already kids
- It's true.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:35.441
- are making fun of this stuff.

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:37.510
So I think that's very healthy.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.846
- But doesn't this seem
a little unique in that

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:42.749 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's like a runaway train
aspect to it right now?

00:04:42.749 --> 00:04:45.451
Where it used to be
kind of like, oh,

00:04:45.451 --> 00:04:47.887 align:start size:84% position:16%
one person randomly
said one thing,

00:04:47.887 --> 00:04:50.023
and they get taken out,
where now it just feels

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:52.158 align:start size:84% position:16%
like the whole
track got destroyed.

00:04:52.158 --> 00:04:54.927
The train is going off and
everyone's kind of going

00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:56.029
with it at the same time.

00:04:56.029 --> 00:04:58.665
- If we were, if this was 1968,

00:04:58.665 --> 00:05:00.133
we'd be saying the same thing,

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:02.568
and we'd be even more panicked.

00:05:02.568 --> 00:05:05.405 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, I think
right now we're dealing

00:05:05.405 --> 00:05:07.674
with a confluence of events.

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:09.876
You're dealing with, you
know the George Floyd,

00:05:09.876 --> 00:05:11.911
the terrible murder
of George Floyd,

00:05:11.911 --> 00:05:14.547 align:start size:97% position:3%
you're dealing with COVID-19
where nobody has jobs,

00:05:14.547 --> 00:05:16.849 align:start size:81% position:19%
and nobody has
anywhere to go to,

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:18.818
and you're dealing with
all these uncertainties,

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:21.888
and you're dealing with this
incredibly polarizing figure

00:05:21.888 --> 00:05:23.389
named Donald Trump.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:26.993
So that, all of that stuff
I think is a perfect storm.

00:05:26.993 --> 00:05:31.164
So when you're in it, it's
hard to see a way out of it,

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:32.665
and it's hard to see sense.

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:35.902
I do think, again, there are
a lot of bad ideas out there,

00:05:35.902 --> 00:05:40.907 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I do think that, but
again, I'm a moderate person,

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:42.442
I just am.

00:05:42.442 --> 00:05:44.410
I'm naturally moderate, medium.

00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:47.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, when I was on
your show, we were trying

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.682 align:start size:97% position:3%
to figure out what we might
differ on politically.

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:51.017
We couldn't come
up with that much.

00:05:51.017 --> 00:05:52.952
- No, I think you're
a very reasonable,

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:55.521
I've listened to you many
times, and I've listened

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:57.590 align:start size:94% position:6%
to your guests and you're,
you know the truth is,

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.126
you are a reasonable,
fair minded person.

00:06:00.126 --> 00:06:01.394
- Don't tell that to
The New York Times.

00:06:01.394 --> 00:06:04.397
- Well, you know,
again, I think...

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:08.401 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then I find myself
saying, you know,

00:06:08.401 --> 00:06:12.171
sometimes these movements
have to be driven by people

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:15.074 align:start size:97% position:3%
who are true believers,
because what happens is they,

00:06:15.074 --> 00:06:17.777
they push things all the way
over, and then it comes back.

00:06:17.777 --> 00:06:19.879
Americans are not
an extreme people.

00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:21.381
We are not extreme.

00:06:21.381 --> 00:06:23.082
Americans are not socialist.

00:06:23.082 --> 00:06:25.718
Americans are not
super right wing.

00:06:25.718 --> 00:06:27.787
Americans want a better
life for their kids,

00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:29.756
for the most part,
and are too busy

00:06:29.756 --> 00:06:33.025
with their every day thing.

00:06:33.025 --> 00:06:34.994 align:start size:97% position:3%
I gotta take my kid to
baseball and I want to be able

00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:37.463
to afford after school
activities for my kids.

00:06:37.463 --> 00:06:39.165
That's what they
really care about.

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:41.200
I want a little extra cash.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.635 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, if you really
come down to it,

00:06:42.635 --> 00:06:46.005
and I think that they,
the silent majority,

00:06:46.005 --> 00:06:48.975 align:start size:94% position:6%
the most they ever really
do is write letters.

00:06:48.975 --> 00:06:50.309
- Tweet, we tweet
now, come on now.

00:06:50.309 --> 00:06:51.744
- You know, stuff like that,

00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:55.681 align:start size:97% position:3%
but you know, come election
time, a lot of times,

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:59.218 align:start size:97% position:3%
if they feel like their
bottom line is being affected,

00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:03.055
i.e. the way their children
are being raised, educated,

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:06.459
and whether or not they
have a little extra cash

00:07:06.459 --> 00:07:08.594 align:start size:97% position:3%
in their pocket, that that'll
get them to the polls.

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:12.598 align:start position:0%
Otherwise, I got enough for
this, and I got enough for that.

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:14.000
- Yeah, I wanna stick
with the comedy thing

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.635 align:start size:94% position:6%
for a little bit, because
you mentioned how,

00:07:15.635 --> 00:07:17.737 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you're a comic and
you're working at a network

00:07:17.737 --> 00:07:20.039
that's making you censor or
making you question yourself,

00:07:20.039 --> 00:07:21.507
then you're working
at the wrong place.

00:07:21.507 --> 00:07:23.009 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yes.
- Isn't it odd though,

00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:25.311
that for all of us that
are now in the podcast,

00:07:25.311 --> 00:07:27.480 align:start size:88% position:13%
YouTube, whatever you
want to call this thing,

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.817 align:start size:97% position:3%
that now we're watching
ourselves do it to ourselves.

00:07:30.817 --> 00:07:32.285
We're our own bosses.

00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:34.253
I'm not saying you're doing
it and I'm not doing it,

00:07:34.253 --> 00:07:37.056 align:start position:0%
but we know a lot of people
now that are getting in trouble

00:07:37.056 --> 00:07:39.258 align:start size:88% position:13%
because of what's
being said on podcasts,

00:07:39.258 --> 00:07:40.960
and we know that, you
know, Media Matters,

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.063
watches every word that
somebody's gonna say,

00:07:44.063 --> 00:07:47.366
so that if you know, anybody
says one thing, the wrong way,

00:07:47.366 --> 00:07:48.601 align:start size:84% position:16%
you can take them out,
- But you're not...

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:50.203
- even though these
guys own their stuff.

00:07:50.203 --> 00:07:53.172
- Yeah, but trouble, when
you say getting in trouble,

00:07:53.172 --> 00:07:57.176
the truth is that they're not.

00:07:57.176 --> 00:07:59.545
The truth is, I look
at getting in trouble

00:07:59.545 --> 00:08:00.713
as not being able
to earn a living.

00:08:00.713 --> 00:08:04.317 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, corporations
are incredibly,

00:08:04.317 --> 00:08:07.153 align:start size:88% position:13%
in many ways,
spineless, and we'll get

00:08:07.153 --> 00:08:08.821
into that in a second,
and what I mean by that,

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:12.892
but I think that
you're still, look,

00:08:12.892 --> 00:08:14.126
I'm on stage all the time.

00:08:14.126 --> 00:08:15.962
I was in Arizona,
I was in Texas.

00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:20.032
Anywhere I go, when
I, when I say things

00:08:20.032 --> 00:08:25.037
that seem reasonable, when
I criticize the sort of,

00:08:26.372 --> 00:08:27.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
even whether it's
conservatives or liberals,

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:29.742 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I criticize
both, people love it.

00:08:29.742 --> 00:08:32.078
People clap, people laugh,

00:08:32.078 --> 00:08:35.581
because typically I
think I always find

00:08:35.581 --> 00:08:38.684
that if I'm feeling this way,
so is most of the country.

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:40.152 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- Because most people

00:08:40.152 --> 00:08:42.121
are just, they're
just reasonable.

00:08:42.121 --> 00:08:45.291 align:start size:94% position:6%
They're not indoctrinated
into an ideology.

00:08:45.291 --> 00:08:48.828
That is, I think the danger
in that is that if you look

00:08:48.828 --> 00:08:50.897
at a lot of the media,
and you look at a lot

00:08:50.897 --> 00:08:54.901
of the cognitive elite, if
you want to call 'em that,

00:08:54.901 --> 00:08:57.770
they, you know, I think it
was Sam Harris who said,

00:08:57.770 --> 00:09:00.306 align:start size:84% position:16%
"I don't know
anybody who smokes."

00:09:00.306 --> 00:09:01.507
Camille Paglia said, I don't,

00:09:01.507 --> 00:09:03.009
"Most of these people
don't know anybody

00:09:03.009 --> 00:09:05.511
"who listens to sport radio,
or works with their hands,

00:09:05.511 --> 00:09:07.246
"or has been in the military."

00:09:07.246 --> 00:09:08.848
That's a big problem.

00:09:08.848 --> 00:09:11.884
That's a huge part
of the country and
no, they're not dumb.

00:09:11.884 --> 00:09:13.319
No, they're not.

00:09:13.319 --> 00:09:17.023 align:start size:97% position:3%
They're just as smart as
anybody in the CNN boardroom,

00:09:17.023 --> 00:09:20.526
or in the Huffington Post
board room, or at Fox News.

00:09:20.526 --> 00:09:22.595
They're just as smart,
I promise you they are.

00:09:22.595 --> 00:09:23.863 align:start size:94% position:6%
- They're probably smarter
- I know all of 'em,

00:09:23.863 --> 00:09:25.298
- in lot of ways.
- I know 'em all.

00:09:25.298 --> 00:09:26.866 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah, and I've been
around, I've been around,

00:09:26.866 --> 00:09:30.403
I'm a little too old now,
and I've talked to probably

00:09:30.403 --> 00:09:32.405
with my podcast, my old podcast,

00:09:32.405 --> 00:09:36.542
I bet I've spoken to, I don't
know, I'll say 400 academics.

00:09:36.542 --> 00:09:38.477
Maybe I'm wrong,
but a lot of 'em,

00:09:38.477 --> 00:09:40.246
and from really
big universities,

00:09:40.246 --> 00:09:41.480
'cause they, I would,
you know, they'd read,

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:42.515 align:start size:84% position:16%
they'd write a book,
and I'd read it,

00:09:42.515 --> 00:09:43.816
and I'd interview them.

