00:18:22.701 --> 00:18:25.971 align:start size:97% position:3%
are now actually much harsher
on illegal immigration

00:18:25.971 --> 00:18:27.473
than your average white liberal.

00:18:27.473 --> 00:18:28.807
- What a surprise.
- Because they came

00:18:28.807 --> 00:18:30.109
in the right way

00:18:30.109 --> 00:18:31.577 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they built.
- They're conservative people.

00:18:31.577 --> 00:18:33.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, and they've become
the new conservatives.

00:18:33.445 --> 00:18:35.080
- They make up a lot
of the school boards

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:36.748 align:start size:84% position:16%
in this country, and
they're Catholic,

00:18:36.748 --> 00:18:38.417 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they're conservative,
and you know,

00:18:38.417 --> 00:18:40.819
they're very, they're
tight knit families,

00:18:40.819 --> 00:18:42.321
and stuff like that, yeah.

00:18:42.321 --> 00:18:43.789
- I'm starting to think
that maybe judging people

00:18:43.789 --> 00:18:45.491 align:start size:94% position:6%
by the color of their
skin and their ethnicity,

00:18:45.491 --> 00:18:46.692
maybe that can't hold

00:18:46.692 --> 00:18:47.926 align:start size:81% position:19%
in the long term.
- Dave, Dave,

00:18:47.926 --> 00:18:49.862 align:start size:84% position:16%
you mean treat people
like individuals?

00:18:49.862 --> 00:18:52.464 align:start size:97% position:3%
You mean the principles
this country was founded on?

00:18:53.866 --> 00:18:55.968 align:start size:97% position:3%
Look, we've always been,
we've always been a country,

00:18:55.968 --> 00:18:59.004
that is, we are a contradiction.

00:18:59.004 --> 00:19:03.942 align:start size:97% position:3%
We've been doing this racial
dance and Jamie Masada,

00:19:03.942 --> 00:19:05.477
who's the owner of
the Laugh Factory,

00:19:05.477 --> 00:19:08.881
told me an amazing story about
when Richard Pryor showed up,

00:19:08.881 --> 00:19:10.182
this is in the 70s.

00:19:10.182 --> 00:19:11.383
Paul Mooney was there.

00:19:11.383 --> 00:19:15.053
Paul had a big afro
and Paul Mooney,

00:19:15.053 --> 00:19:16.989
Richard Pryor came in
with his crazy Mercedes

00:19:16.989 --> 00:19:18.323
and Paul Mooney, he goes,
"What are you doing, bro?

00:19:18.323 --> 00:19:20.425
"That's a white man's
car, are you crazy?"

00:19:20.425 --> 00:19:22.861 align:start size:97% position:3%
And Richard Pryor said,
"What are you talking about?"

00:19:22.861 --> 00:19:24.963
And he said, "Get in the car."

00:19:24.963 --> 00:19:26.999
So Paul Mooney gets in the car.

00:19:26.999 --> 00:19:28.100
He says, "I'm driving."

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:29.635
So Paul had this afro.

00:19:29.635 --> 00:19:31.737 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's Richard Pryor,
it's two other comics,

00:19:31.737 --> 00:19:33.805 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think one was
black as well, maybe,

00:19:33.805 --> 00:19:35.874 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then Jamie
Masada in the back,

00:19:35.874 --> 00:19:39.077
and they take a drive, and
they got stopped four times

00:19:39.077 --> 00:19:44.082
for "swerving," and finally,
a black cop in Beverly Hills

00:19:45.484 --> 00:19:46.685 align:start size:97% position:3%
said, "You're Richard Pryor,
he's the greatest thing

00:19:46.685 --> 00:19:48.220
"in the world," and
they let him go.

00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:50.923
But they went to Brentwood,
they went to Beverly Hills,

00:19:50.923 --> 00:19:54.326
and so it was, there is no
doubt that historically,

00:19:54.326 --> 00:19:56.128 align:start size:88% position:13%
there is no doubt if
you're African American,

00:19:56.128 --> 00:19:58.430 align:start size:97% position:3%
you have a different
relationship with the police

00:19:58.430 --> 00:19:59.631
than you do if you're white.

00:19:59.631 --> 00:20:00.832
There's no doubt about that.

00:20:00.832 --> 00:20:02.801 align:start size:88% position:13%
I would never argue
that in a million years.

00:20:02.801 --> 00:20:07.005
The question is, the question
is, what do we do about it?

00:20:07.005 --> 00:20:07.940
What do we do about it?

00:20:07.940 --> 00:20:09.942
Dismantle the police force?

00:20:09.942 --> 00:20:11.510 align:start size:88% position:13%
So these are the,
these are the bad ideas

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:13.445 align:start size:88% position:13%
that I think are, these
reactionary ideas.

00:20:13.445 --> 00:20:14.846
- So the billion they're cuttin'

00:20:14.846 --> 00:20:18.150 align:start size:97% position:3%
from New York City policing,
that de Blasio wants

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:21.019 align:start size:94% position:6%
to get rid of as, as crime
is now, I think murders

00:20:21.019 --> 00:20:22.921 line:0%
are up 150%, last week,
- Shootings are, yeah.

00:20:22.921 --> 00:20:24.423 line:0%
- or something.
- That'll be

00:20:24.423 --> 00:20:27.392 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
his political liability
and de Blasio is not a man

00:20:27.392 --> 00:20:29.628 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
who lives in reality,
in my opinion.

00:20:29.628 --> 00:20:31.930
And I think he's a
guy who, you know,

00:20:31.930 --> 00:20:33.165
he was talking about how,

00:20:33.165 --> 00:20:35.534
I don't think he's
ever had a real,

00:20:35.534 --> 00:20:37.569
I don't think he's
ever had a real job.

00:20:37.569 --> 00:20:42.541
And he'll tell you if he was
here, he'd say I run a company

00:20:43.442 --> 00:20:44.977
that's got 50,000 employees.

00:20:44.977 --> 00:20:46.211
I know, I know Bill.

00:20:46.211 --> 00:20:47.879 align:start size:81% position:19%
I know, that's
good, good on you,

00:20:47.879 --> 00:20:49.414
except for you don't
have to turn a profit.

00:20:49.414 --> 00:20:50.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Right.
And it doesn't mean

00:20:50.382 --> 00:20:51.583
you run it well..

00:20:51.583 --> 00:20:52.818 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And it doesn't
mean you run it well.

00:20:52.818 --> 00:20:55.320 align:start size:88% position:13%
And he's got a very
strong, it seems to me,

00:20:55.320 --> 00:20:57.356
from what I've heard,
a socialist ideology.

00:20:57.356 --> 00:21:00.192
He speaks like a lot of
politicians did in Sweden

00:21:00.192 --> 00:21:03.929 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the 70s, and let's
see what happens.

00:21:03.929 --> 00:21:04.863
Let's see what happens.

00:21:04.863 --> 00:21:06.331
I'm not impressed so far.

00:21:06.331 --> 00:21:09.601
I do think that police
unions are very powerful.

00:21:09.601 --> 00:21:11.670 align:start size:88% position:13%
I do think they got,
they get a lot of money,

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:14.439
to the tune of sometimes
they get too much money

00:21:14.439 --> 00:21:16.742
in terms of the tax
base in those towns,

00:21:16.742 --> 00:21:19.378
like Stockton, can't afford

00:21:19.378 --> 00:21:23.615 align:start size:97% position:3%
to support the fireman safety
union, the police union.

00:21:23.615 --> 00:21:25.083
Michael Lewis wrote a good book,

00:21:25.083 --> 00:21:27.019
highlighting this in a
book called, "Boomerang."

00:21:27.019 --> 00:21:29.855 align:start size:97% position:3%
I recommend it to people,
and he highlights California.

00:21:29.855 --> 00:21:32.157 align:start size:81% position:19%
So, sometimes when
you're talking

00:21:32.157 --> 00:21:33.492
about defunding the police,

00:21:33.492 --> 00:21:35.227 align:start size:84% position:16%
you're talking about
taking money away

00:21:35.227 --> 00:21:37.029 align:start size:88% position:13%
from those very powerful
unions and putting it

00:21:37.029 --> 00:21:38.697
into other programs.

00:21:38.697 --> 00:21:39.865
I understand that.

00:21:39.865 --> 00:21:43.068 align:start size:88% position:13%
Again, again, you
know, when I dismiss it

00:21:43.068 --> 00:21:44.803 align:start size:84% position:16%
as a liberal argument,
I'm not being fair.

00:21:44.803 --> 00:21:46.672
There are very smart
people on the left,

00:21:46.672 --> 00:21:48.974
who are looking at data as well,

00:21:48.974 --> 00:21:51.476 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they have
something to offer.

00:21:51.476 --> 00:21:53.578
So I don't want to, you know.

00:21:53.578 --> 00:21:55.814 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, well look, you
know where I stand on this,

00:21:55.814 --> 00:21:57.549
and it's like, if you told
me you were cutting budgets,

00:21:57.549 --> 00:21:59.885 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you told me you were
cutting, basically every city,

00:21:59.885 --> 00:22:02.220 align:start size:88% position:13%
federal state, the whole
thing, budgets by 20%

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:04.623
across the board, I think,
I think we'd be just fine.

00:22:04.623 --> 00:22:07.392
I don't think when you're
looking at 150% increases

00:22:07.392 --> 00:22:09.661
in murder, that you, that
what you should be doing,

00:22:09.661 --> 00:22:13.699
optically, if, if not even
granularly or correctly,

00:22:13.699 --> 00:22:15.634 align:start size:97% position:3%
you should be doing it, 'cause
it just doesn't look right.

00:22:15.634 --> 00:22:18.337 align:start size:97% position:3%
But you mentioned something
to me right before we started.

00:22:18.337 --> 00:22:20.906
I had Giuliani on a
couple of weeks ago,

00:22:20.906 --> 00:22:24.209 align:start position:0%
and when you say that de Blasio
is, you know not qualified,

00:22:24.209 --> 00:22:25.711
or it's like, what
has he done to prove

00:22:25.711 --> 00:22:27.179
that he can run this city?

00:22:27.179 --> 00:22:28.947 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well, Giuliani, the
guy has got a pedigree.

00:22:28.947 --> 00:22:31.950 align:start size:97% position:3%
He was an attorney general,
he was hunting down the mafia.

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:34.286
- Well his father was, got
shook down by the mafia.

00:22:34.286 --> 00:22:35.821
- Right, so he talks about that.

00:22:35.821 --> 00:22:39.624 align:start size:97% position:3%
So his whole life in many
ways led to being the mayor,

00:22:39.624 --> 00:22:42.094
the guy in charge of
the most important city

00:22:42.094 --> 00:22:43.729 align:start size:88% position:13%
during the most horrific
terrorist attack,

00:22:43.729 --> 00:22:45.230
the whole thing.

00:22:45.230 --> 00:22:46.798
You were telling me a little
bit about what it was like

00:22:46.798 --> 00:22:48.734
to be in New York City.

00:22:48.734 --> 00:22:50.635
You got, you got a
couple years on me,

00:22:50.635 --> 00:22:52.104 align:start size:94% position:6%
so there were some things
that you remembered

00:22:52.104 --> 00:22:53.638 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I don't quite remember.
- I remember New York.

00:22:53.638 --> 00:22:55.307
My mother and father, my
mother was born and raised

00:22:55.307 --> 00:22:56.975 align:start size:84% position:16%
in Brooklyn, you know,
and then she went

00:22:56.975 --> 00:22:59.745 align:start size:84% position:16%
to Westchester
County, but my family,

00:22:59.745 --> 00:23:01.213 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Italian side was...
- My dad, born in Brooklyn,

00:23:01.213 --> 00:23:02.147
my mom grew up in Westchester.

00:23:02.147 --> 00:23:03.548
- All right, well good.

00:23:03.548 --> 00:23:07.152 align:start position:0%
So the Italian side, the
Sicilian side are all Brooklyn,

00:23:07.152 --> 00:23:10.722
Staten Island, and then
some in Westchester.

00:23:10.722 --> 00:23:14.693
So I would always, I grew
up overseas my whole life,

00:23:14.693 --> 00:23:18.063
but when I finally went to
college in Washington, DC,

00:23:18.063 --> 00:23:20.132
I would come up to New
York to see my family,

00:23:20.132 --> 00:23:21.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I had my
friends in New York.

00:23:21.833 --> 00:23:25.103 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so I remember
what New York was like

00:23:25.103 --> 00:23:29.174
in '87, '88, '89, '90.

00:23:29.174 --> 00:23:31.710
When you went down to
Tompkins Square Park,

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:34.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
when they, when they shut
it down and redid it,

00:23:34.513 --> 00:23:37.949
they found diseases in
the soil like tetanus,

00:23:37.949 --> 00:23:39.785
like bubonic, plague.

00:23:39.785 --> 00:23:44.756 align:start position:0%
I mean you, when my grandfather
found out I was hanging out

00:23:44.756 --> 00:23:47.926
on Avenue A, I was
dating Patty Jenkins,

00:23:47.926 --> 00:23:50.128
who is the great director of
"Wonder Woman," (mumbling),

00:23:50.128 --> 00:23:53.131 align:start size:88% position:13%
and she lived on Avenue
A and 11th street.

00:23:53.131 --> 00:23:55.400
And when I would
go down there, man,

00:23:55.400 --> 00:23:58.503
I was always so nervous
because it was Mad Max,

00:23:58.503 --> 00:24:00.305
it was chaos, it was crazy.

00:24:00.305 --> 00:24:03.074
And my grandfather couldn't
believe I was hanging out

00:24:03.074 --> 00:24:05.877
down there, and there was
an expression back then,

00:24:05.877 --> 00:24:07.879
if you hung out an Avenue
A, you're adventurous,

00:24:07.879 --> 00:24:11.082
B, you were bold, C you were
crazy, and D you were dead.

00:24:11.082 --> 00:24:14.152 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I'm telling you, man, when
I used to go see my buddy,

00:24:14.152 --> 00:24:16.888 align:start size:97% position:3%
John Joseph, who was the
lead singer of the Cro Mags,

00:24:16.888 --> 00:24:21.326
this is in 1989, this
was probably '91, '91.

00:24:21.326 --> 00:24:23.795
People don't believe
this, but it was '91.

00:24:23.795 --> 00:24:25.263
It was right where CBGBs was.

00:24:25.263 --> 00:24:29.234
I could not, he would have
to meet me on Houston Street,

00:24:29.234 --> 00:24:32.404
because we would go into,
like where Ludlow Street,

00:24:32.404 --> 00:24:34.206
Rivington, try buying
an apartment there now.

00:24:34.206 --> 00:24:35.307
See how much it costs you.

00:24:35.307 --> 00:24:36.541
Forget it.

00:24:36.541 --> 00:24:37.476
I mean, you can't
look at a place

00:24:37.476 --> 00:24:39.144
for under $3,000 a square foot.

00:24:39.144 --> 00:24:40.812 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, it's a little
less now because the city

00:24:40.812 --> 00:24:42.614
ain't doing so great, but...
- Okay, maybe a little less,

00:24:42.614 --> 00:24:43.849
maybe a little less.

00:24:43.849 --> 00:24:46.718
I was, I could not,
he had to walk me

00:24:46.718 --> 00:24:49.254 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I'd get jumped
by the local kids,

00:24:49.254 --> 00:24:51.156
the gangs that were
hanging out there,

00:24:51.156 --> 00:24:54.292
and I would walk with him, but
if I wasn't walking with him,

00:24:54.292 --> 00:24:55.427
I was in trouble.

00:24:55.427 --> 00:24:56.828
And he was adamant about that.

00:24:56.828 --> 00:24:58.230
He goes, "You can't, you
gotta, I gotta meet you.

00:24:58.230 --> 00:24:59.297
"I'm just afraid that, you know,

00:24:59.297 --> 00:25:00.665
"somebody will mess with you."

00:25:00.665 --> 00:25:03.568
So to see that radical
shift under Giuliani

00:25:03.568 --> 00:25:04.769
was kind of amazing.

00:25:04.769 --> 00:25:06.371
Look, I don't know
what it's like to run

00:25:06.371 --> 00:25:08.440
and be the mayor of a city.

00:25:08.440 --> 00:25:10.242
I don't know Bill de Blasio,

00:25:10.242 --> 00:25:12.077
I don't know what the
challenges he faces.

00:25:12.077 --> 00:25:13.912
I do have a sense of
what his ideology is,

00:25:13.912 --> 00:25:16.548
and I don't think it's the
best thing in the world for,

00:25:16.548 --> 00:25:18.416
we are in very different...

00:25:18.416 --> 00:25:20.352 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Do you consider
yourself that political?

00:25:20.352 --> 00:25:22.087
It's interesting 'cause
you're sort of political,

00:25:22.087 --> 00:25:24.523 align:start size:84% position:16%
but also it strikes
me as more, just like,

00:25:24.523 --> 00:25:25.724
- Look...

00:25:25.724 --> 00:25:27.158 align:start size:81% position:19%
- I care about
life or something.

00:25:27.158 --> 00:25:30.862 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I think Albert Camus
said, "In the 20th century,

00:25:30.862 --> 00:25:33.031
"no one can afford
to not be politically

00:25:33.031 --> 00:25:34.966
"and philosophically committed."

00:25:34.966 --> 00:25:36.868 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the 20th century
saw what happens

00:25:36.868 --> 00:25:40.105 align:start size:88% position:13%
when you're on the
wrong side of the coin.

00:25:40.105 --> 00:25:44.109
When, when bad ideas win,

00:25:44.109 --> 00:25:48.680 align:start position:0%
when bad ideas like collectivism
and fascism win the day,

00:25:50.782 --> 00:25:53.251
people die, people die.

00:25:53.251 --> 00:25:57.822 align:start position:0%
And I can take you to 80 million
graves to prove that point.

00:25:57.822 --> 00:26:02.294 align:start size:97% position:3%
See the 40 year European
Civil War called World War I

00:26:02.294 --> 00:26:07.299 align:start size:97% position:3%
and World War II, and the
red, and the famines in China,

00:26:08.867 --> 00:26:11.536 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the famines and
forced collectivization

00:26:11.536 --> 00:26:14.806
in Stalin's Russia,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:26:14.806 --> 00:26:18.143
So my favorite expression is,
"You may not be interested

00:26:18.143 --> 00:26:19.678 align:start size:84% position:16%
"in politics, but it's
interested in you,

00:26:19.678 --> 00:26:21.179
"and it will come for you."

00:26:21.179 --> 00:26:23.949
So yes, I'm political and
yes, I'm philosophical,

00:26:23.949 --> 00:26:26.151
and it's very difficult
to earn your opinion.

00:26:26.151 --> 00:26:27.886
It's difficult, it
takes a lot of time,

00:26:27.886 --> 00:26:30.088 align:start size:94% position:6%
it takes a lot of reading
and it requires you

00:26:30.088 --> 00:26:31.923
to change your mind
and stay humble.

00:26:31.923 --> 00:26:35.327 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I guess I am political,
but I don't know.

00:26:35.327 --> 00:26:39.698
I try to be, I try to
be middle of the road.

00:26:39.698 --> 00:26:41.399
Well, I, no.

00:26:41.399 --> 00:26:43.868
A better way to say it
is I try to be responsive

00:26:43.868 --> 00:26:45.370
to the evidence.

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:48.006
I try to really listen to
people I disagree with.

00:26:48.006 --> 00:26:50.475
I do, and I get a lot out of it.

00:26:50.475 --> 00:26:53.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
I try to listen to people
who are, for example,

00:26:54.746 --> 00:26:59.384
the liberal, I've read a
lot of Ta-Nehisi Coates,

00:26:59.384 --> 00:27:01.987 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Ta-Nehisi is, you
know, would be considered

00:27:01.987 --> 00:27:03.521
on the sort of left
side of the spectrum

00:27:03.521 --> 00:27:05.957
in black intelligentsia.

00:27:05.957 --> 00:27:07.425
He's a beautiful writer, man.

00:27:07.425 --> 00:27:10.595 align:start size:97% position:3%
He writes, it's almost like
these psalms he writes.

00:27:10.595 --> 00:27:11.863
They're so moving.

00:27:11.863 --> 00:27:13.932
God, I mean that guy's
an incredible writer.

00:27:13.932 --> 00:27:15.967 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's okay to be really
moved by something,

00:27:15.967 --> 00:27:19.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
and have it emotionally
move you, and to believe it,

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:20.772
